## 1. Objective and scope

Determine why teams following the same nominal workplace process exhibit persistent cross-site differences in completion time.

Treat the process as a distributed software system:

- The written process is the **specification**.
- Each site’s enacted workflow is an **implementation**.
- Cases are **test inputs**.
- Completion time and intermediate events are **outputs and traces**.
- Site differences are potential **implementation defects, configuration differences, environmental effects, or input-distribution effects**.

The study will:

1. Establish whether site differences persist after accounting for case mix and calendar exposure.
2. Test competing explanations:
   - different incoming work;
   - divergent process implementation;
   - staffing, workload, or resource constraints;
   - delays at handoffs and external dependencies;
   - tooling or configuration differences;
   - strategic batching, prioritization, or queue discipline;
   - measurement artifacts;
   - interactions among these factors.
3. Identify observations that discriminate among explanations.
4. Estimate effects on both total completion time and stage-level delay.

Primary outcome: elapsed time from a prespecified start event to a prespecified completion event.  
Secondary outcomes: active processing time, queue time, rework, failure or exception frequency, handoff delay, and deadline attainment.

## 2. Assumptions

- The process has definable start, completion, and intermediate states.
- Sites perform sufficiently comparable work to support comparison; non-overlapping case types will be excluded or analyzed separately.
- Event timestamps and case identifiers can be standardized and audited.
- Completion time may be right-censored for unfinished cases and influenced by weekends, holidays, shifts, and time zones.
- The nominal process may under-specify local configuration, exceptions, or workarounds.
- Cases may be clustered within workers, teams, customers, and sites.
- Site assignment may not be random, so observational comparisons alone do not establish causality.
- Process changes may occur during the study and must be versioned.
- “Faster” is not necessarily “better”; quality, rework, compliance, and downstream failures must be measured as guardrails.
- No individual-level performance conclusions will be drawn unless measurement validity, task comparability, and ethical review support them.

Before data collection, preregister the outcome definitions, exclusion rules, hypotheses, model families, minimum detectable effects, and stopping rules.

## 3. Research design

### A. Build an executable process specification

Translate the nominal procedure into a testable model containing:

- required and optional states;
- permitted transitions;
- roles authorized for each action;
- prerequisites and validation rules;
- exception paths;
- expected service-time or service-level bounds;
- start/stop rules for clocks;
- tool versions and configuration requirements.

Assign stable versions to the specification and to each site implementation. Resolve genuine policy ambiguity before treating deviations as defects.

### B. Baseline observational study

Collect a prospective, multisite cohort over complete operational cycles, supplemented by historical records only if their definitions are compatible.

For each case, reconstruct the event trace and compare it with the executable specification. Run the equivalent of:

- **conformance tests:** required steps, ordering, permissions, and validations;
- **unit tests:** stage-level processing behavior;
- **integration tests:** handoffs among roles, systems, and external parties;
- **load tests:** behavior under varying queue lengths and arrival rates;
- **regression tests:** whether updates altered timing or quality;
- **boundary tests:** deadlines, shift changes, month-end periods, and unusual case sizes;
- **fault-path tests:** missing information, rejection, escalation, and rework;
- **configuration-differential tests:** identical logical tasks under documented site configurations.

This phase estimates adjusted site differences and localizes them to stages and trace patterns.

### C. Construct competing explanatory models

Test the following prespecified hypotheses and signatures:

| Explanation | Predicted observations | Observation distinguishing it |
|---|---|---|
| Case mix | Slow sites receive more complex or incomplete cases | Site effect shrinks after balancing on detailed pre-start attributes |
| Process divergence | Sites follow different paths or repeat steps | Trace variants and conformance violations explain the gap |
| Capacity/load | Delays rise with queue length, utilization, absence, or arrival bursts | Within-site completion time changes with load; active work time remains similar |
| Skill/experience | Differences concentrate by role or tenure and in active processing or errors | Similar-load workers differ by experience; training changes subsequent performance without reducing quality |
| Handoff/dependency | Waiting concentrates between owners or at external interfaces | Gap is largely queue time at specific transitions |
| Tool/configuration | Timing changes by software version, automation, permissions, or latency | Matched tasks differ by configuration; synchronized changes create discontinuities |
| Batching/prioritization | Work accumulates and clears periodically; classes are reordered | Sawtooth queue patterns and long idle intervals precede short processing bursts |
| Measurement artifact | Clocks or event semantics differ | Audit observations disagree systematically with system timestamps |
| Quality-speed tradeoff | Faster sites omit checks or create downstream work | Shorter time predicts more defects, rework, or compliance failures |
| Unobserved local context | Residual site effect remains after measured mechanisms | Repeated matched tests and audits fail to account for the stable gap |

### D. Controlled discriminating tests

After baseline analysis, use staged, minimally disruptive interventions where feasible:

1. **Synthetic or standardized cases:** Submit equivalent, clearly labeled test cases to participating sites under approved conditions. Vary one input feature at a time and include boundary and exception cases. Do not mix them with real decisions where that would create operational or ethical risk.
2. **Replay or simulation in a non-production environment:** Run identical cases through site configurations to isolate tooling and validation differences.
3. **Randomized diagnostic changes:** Randomize eligible teams or time blocks to a single reversible change, such as standardized intake, a handoff alert, queue visibility, or configuration alignment.
4. **Stepped-wedge rollout:** If all sites must eventually receive a change, randomize adoption order.
5. **Matched natural experiments:** Analyze unplanned version deployments, outages, staffing changes, or policy updates using interrupted time-series or difference-in-differences designs.

Change one mechanism at a time when possible. Require treatment-fidelity checks and retain quality guardrails.

### E. Replication

Replicate key findings:

- across at least two time periods;
- across common case strata;
- after excluding exceptional operating periods;
- with both recorded logs and an independent audit sample;
- on sites or teams not used to identify the initial explanation, where feasible.

## 4. Data or evidence needed

### Case and timing data

- anonymized case identifier;
- site, team, role, and responsible unit;
- arrival, eligibility, start, stage-transition, handoff, completion, cancellation, and reopen timestamps;
- stage owner and status;
- deadlines and service-level targets;
- complete/incomplete/censored status.

### Input and case-mix data

Measured before work begins:

- case type, size, complexity, urgency, and priority;
- completeness and data-quality indicators;
- customer or requestor characteristics relevant to processing;
- required approvals, dependencies, and exception flags;
- routing and assignment mechanism.

### Process-trace data

- ordered event logs with actor, system, and source;
- process path and skipped, repeated, or out-of-order steps;
- rework, rejection, escalation, and reopen events;
- local workarounds and manual actions;
- batch sizes and queue-selection decisions.

### Capacity and environment data

- staffing by role and shift;
- worker tenure and relevant training;
- scheduled hours, absence, overtime, and turnover;
- arrival rates, backlog, queue length, and work in progress;
- holidays, operating hours, time zones, and peak periods;
- external-provider response times.

### Technology and configuration data

- software, workflow, and policy versions;
- permissions and automation settings;
- integration latency, outages, errors, retries, and system performance;
- deployment dates and site-specific configuration;
- hardware or connectivity constraints where relevant.

### Quality and compliance data

- downstream defects;
- audit failures;
- customer complaints;
- rework and reopen rates;
- omissions and policy deviations;
- consequential errors or adverse outcomes.

### Validation evidence

- structured interviews and direct observations using a common protocol;
- timestamp audits against source records;
- specification and configuration reviews;
- logs from standardized test cases;
- intervention assignment and adherence records.

Collect only necessary data, minimize identifiers, impose access controls and retention limits, and prevent punitive use of exploratory worker-level results.

## 5. Measurement

### Completion-time decomposition

For case \(i\):

\[
T_i = T_{active,i} + T_{queue,i} + T_{handoff,i} + T_{external,i} + T_{rework,i}
\]

Define each component before collection. Report both:

- calendar elapsed time; and
- business-time elapsed time based on a common calendar plus a sensitivity analysis using local operating calendars.

### Primary measures

- time from eligibility/start to valid completion;
- cause-specific time spent in each process state;
- probability of completion by prespecified horizons;
- deadline attainment.

### Explanatory measures

- standardized pre-start complexity score;
- process-conformance score;
- path/trace variant;
- number and duration of handoffs;
- workload and utilization measures;
- staffing-to-arrival ratio;
- tool/configuration indicators;
- batching index;
- rework count and duration.

Avoid conditioning primary causal models on post-start variables unless estimating mediation. Distinguish confounders from mediators and outcomes using a prespecified causal diagram.

### Reliability and validity

- Harmonize timestamp semantics and time zones.
- Test duplicate, missing, impossible, and out-of-order events.
- Estimate inter-rater reliability for manual complexity and audit coding.
- Compare log timestamps with observed or source-record timestamps in a stratified sample.
- Blind auditors to site performance rankings where feasible.
- Analyze missingness by site and process stage.
- Version all variable definitions and transformation code.
- Retain raw immutable logs and create reproducible derived datasets.

### Sampling and power

Base sample size on the minimum operationally meaningful difference in median or restricted mean completion time, accounting for:

- site and team clustering;
- censoring;
- unequal site sizes;
- repeated periods;
- expected intervention adherence;
- multiplicity across key mechanisms.

Ensure adequate representation of common case types at each site. Rare case types will be pooled only when their process paths are substantively comparable.

## 6. Analysis

### A. Descriptive and diagnostic analysis

- Plot arrival, completion, backlog, and stage-residence distributions by site and period.
- Use medians, quantiles, restricted mean completion time, and full distributions rather than means alone.
- Visualize process variants, transition frequencies, rework loops, and bottlenecks.
- Compare active time with waiting time.
- Check whether site rankings are stable over time and case strata.
- Examine missingness, clock anomalies, and logging differences before substantive inference.

### B. Adjusted estimation

Fit multilevel time-to-event models with cases nested within workers or teams and sites. Depending on diagnostics, use accelerated failure-time, flexible parametric survival, or multistate models.

Include:

- pre-start case characteristics;
- calendar and shift effects;
- assignment and routing factors;
- site and team effects;
- time-varying workload;
- prespecified interactions.

Use partial pooling to avoid overinterpreting small sites. Report adjusted contrasts with uncertainty intervals and absolute operational differences.

Use overlap weighting, matching, or standardization to compare sites only on shared case-mix support. Flag extrapolations and conduct a restricted-overlap analysis.

### C. Mechanism discrimination

Estimate models sequentially, not as proof from coefficient attenuation but as descriptive localization:

1. unadjusted site differences;
2. adjustment for pre-start case mix;
3. addition of calendar and assignment factors;
4. decomposition by process stage;
5. mediation analysis for trace, queue, handoff, and configuration mechanisms where assumptions are defensible.

Predicted discriminators include:

- **Case mix supported:** balanced or standardized comparisons substantially reduce the gap, including on independent validation data.
- **Queue/capacity supported:** delay is primarily inactive time and responds to exogenous or randomized load/capacity changes.
- **Process divergence supported:** specific trace deviations precede delay and correcting them reduces delay without harming quality.
- **Tooling supported:** identical inputs diverge by configuration, and configuration alignment changes timing.
- **Measurement artifact supported:** audited timing removes the apparent difference.
- **Quality tradeoff supported:** faster completion is offset by higher failure, rework, or downstream delay.
- **Residual context:** stable differences survive adjustment, standardized cases, trace analysis, and measurement audits.

### D. Intervention analysis

Analyze randomized tests by intention to treat and, secondarily, by adherence-aware methods. For natural experiments:

- verify pretrends;
- model site-specific seasonality;
- use unaffected sites or stages as controls;
- test for anticipation and concurrent changes;
- report event-time estimates.

Require evidence that the intervention changed the targeted mechanism. For example, a handoff alert should reduce handoff waiting before attributing total-time improvement to handoff management.

### E. Robustness and falsification

- Alternative start and completion definitions.
- Calendar versus business time.
- Different complexity specifications.
- Exclusion of outages, peak periods, and extreme cases.
- Models accounting for competing events such as cancellation.
- Negative-control outcomes or stages that the proposed mechanism should not affect.
- Placebo intervention dates.
- Leave-one-site-out and holdout-period validation.
- Sensitivity analysis for unmeasured confounding and informative censoring.
- Correction or hierarchical control for multiple mechanism tests.

## 7. Decision / stopping rule

Classify an explanation as **supported** only if all prespecified conditions hold:

1. It predicts where and when delay occurs.
2. The relevant mechanism measure differs across sites.
3. The mechanism temporally precedes the delay.
4. Adjustment, standardized testing, or intervention changes the site contrast in the predicted direction.
5. The result replicates in a holdout period, independent site, or repeated intervention.
6. Quality and compliance guardrails do not show a practically important deterioration.
7. Plausible rival explanations and measurement artifacts have been tested.

Classify an explanation as:

- **partially supported** when evidence is consistent but lacks causal discrimination or replication;
- **not supported** when predicted signatures are absent with adequately precise estimates;
- **inconclusive** when data quality, overlap, adherence, power, or identification is insufficient.

Use both statistical and practical thresholds. Before analysis, define:

- the minimum meaningful reduction in completion time;
- the maximum acceptable increase in defects, rework, or compliance failures;
- equivalence bounds for declaring effects operationally negligible;
- required replication criteria.

Stop data collection when the planned information size is reached, all primary diagnostic tests have valid data, and precision is adequate to distinguish meaningful effects from the equivalence bounds. Permit early termination only for prespecified safety, compliance, severe quality, or data-integrity concerns—not because interim results are favorable.

If substantial residual site variation remains after the planned tests, conclude that the current evidence does not identify the cause and specify the unresolved observations needed; do not select the best-fitting explanation by default.

## 8. Limitations

- Observational site comparisons remain vulnerable to unmeasured case mix, assignment, and local context.
- Standardized test cases may not reproduce the social and operational features of real cases.
- Instrumentation can alter behavior, and sites may temporarily conform more closely when observed.
- Event logs may record system actions rather than actual human work.
- Queue time and active time can be difficult to separate when work occurs outside tracked systems.
- Tool, policy, staffing, and demand changes may coincide, complicating attribution.
- Interventions may spill over between workers or alter prioritization of untreated cases.
- Findings may not generalize to rare cases, future process versions, or sites outside the study.
- Mediation estimates require stronger assumptions than total-effect estimates.
- Completion-time improvements may shift work or delay downstream rather than eliminate it.
- Privacy and labor-relations constraints may limit worker-level measurements.
- Persistent residual differences should not be interpreted as site or worker capability without validated measures and stronger causal evidence.
